+//! The life of a job, written down once.
+//!
+//! A job is in one of four states ([`JobState`]) and something happens to it ([`Event`]);
+//! [`transition`] is those two sentences written as **one total function**: every pair has an
+//! answer, and the answer for a move the machine does not allow is an [`Illegal`] that names both
+//! sides rather than a panic, a silent no-op or a state nobody expected.
+//!
+//! Six of the twenty-four pairs of a state and an event are moves; the table with those rows is
+//! in the step's body, and [`transition`] is where they are written down once. Every other pair is
+//! refused, and the refusal says which of the three things went wrong ([`Reason`]): the job is
+//! finished, the event needed it to be running, or the event needed it to be waiting. `succeeded`
+//! and `failed` are **terminal** — nothing happens after the end of a job, whatever arrives — and
+//! that is the half of the model a queue lives on: a job that finished must never be handed out
+//! again, and "must never" written as a state is what the other rules are checked against.
+//!
+//! This module is the model, and the model is a value. Three things follow from that, and they are
+//! what `jobq/3.14-job-state-machine` is about:
+//!
+//! * **the destinations are here, not in the settle paths.** Both backends compute where a job
+//!   goes by asking [`transition`], so `queued`, `running`, `succeeded` and `failed` are written
+//!   in one file in this crate. What the *statements* keep is the predicate — whether a row is
+//!   eligible at all — because a predicate that matches nothing is a query with no answer, while a
+//!   destination that a store invented is a row in a state the model does not have;
+//! * **the row's `state` column is the encoding of this enum, never the model.** [`JobState::as_str`]
+//!   and [`JobState::parse`] are the two directions, and a name this build does not know is refused
+//!   by the text that was stored ([`UnknownState`]) rather than rounded down to a state that would
+//!   run the job again. `migrations/0006_job_states.sql` is the same sentence one layer down: the
+//!   column has a `check`, so the database refuses a state the model does not have — from a store,
+//!   from a migration, or from somebody with `psql` open;
+//! * **every move has one answer.** A settle that is refused is [`Error::Invalid`] and stays that
+//!   way (`jobq/3.7-leases-and-recovery` pinned the variant, and a worker reading the message needs
+//!   the lease in it, which the model has no opinion about). What the model adds is that the
+//!   *destination* of every accepted move is one function rather than one per backend, which is
+//!   what a property test can walk (`jobq/3.19-property-invariants`).
+//!
+//! Where the moves come from is the other half: `jobq/3.6-transaction-isolation` is where a settle
+//! became one transaction, `jobq/3.7-leases-and-recovery` is where a claim stops being only a state
+//! change, and the two terminal states arrived with `delivery/dlq@apply` at `relay/2.10-dlq` — a
+//! job that is out of attempts is a job a human has to look at, which is a *state* rather than an
+//! absence of one.
+
+use std::fmt;
+
+use crate::engine::domain::JobState;
+
+/// Something that happens to a job.
+///
+/// The two events a settle produces ([`Event::Complete`], [`Event::Fail`]) and the two a claim
+/// produces ([`Event::Claim`], [`Event::ClaimLapsed`]) are the whole of what a job's life consists
+/// of. An event is *what happened*, not *what should be written down*: `retryable` is the attempt
+/// budget's answer, handed in by the store that counted the attempts, because the machine is about
+/// where a job is and not about how many tries it has had.
+///
+/// `#[non_exhaustive]`, because this list grows: a cancellation, a replay, a purge each arrive as a
+/// move in a later step, and a caller that wrote its wildcard arm on day one does not have to be
+/// rebuilt the day one of them lands (step `jobq/3.2-public-api-baseline`).
+#[non_exhaustive]
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
+pub enum Event {
+    /// A worker took the job.
+    Claim,
+    /// The attempt finished the job.
+    Complete,
+    /// The attempt failed. `retryable` is whether the budget leaves another attempt.
+    Fail {
+        /// Whether another attempt is allowed, as the store's attempt count answered it.
+        retryable: bool,
+    },
+    /// The claim on a running job went away without being settled: the worker handed it back on its
+    /// way out, or its lease ran out and nobody renewed it.
+    ///
+    /// One event for both, and the same `retryable` as [`Event::Fail`], because the *state* answer
+    /// is the same one and the difference between the two is the story the row tells — the reason
+    /// in `last_error`, the kind of line in the trail, and which counter moves
+    /// (`jobq/3.13-queue-authz-attribution` counts them apart). The model's job is where the job
+    /// is; a model that merged them would have hidden which of the two happened.
+    ClaimLapsed {
+        /// Whether another attempt is allowed, as the store's attempt count answered it.
+        retryable: bool,
+    },
+}
+
+impl Event {
+    /// The event's name, as the table in the step's body writes it.
+    ///
+    /// One name per *kind*, not per flag: `retryable` is the budget's answer and it never decides
+    /// whether a move is allowed, so it is not part of what the event is called.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn as_str(self) -> &'static str {
+        todo!("jobq/3.14-job-state-machine: the name this event is written and printed under")
+    }
+}
+
+impl fmt::Display for Event {
+    fn fmt(&self, formatter: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
+        formatter.write_str(self.as_str())
+    }
+}
+
+/// Every state, for the callers that have to be exhaustive rather than optimistic.
+///
+/// A property test walking the model (`jobq/3.19-property-invariants`) needs the list as data, and a
+/// list written by hand is a list that goes stale the moment a state is added: this one is checked
+/// against [`JobState::as_str`]'s names by the step's spec, so a fifth state cannot arrive without
+/// the round trip being looked at.
+pub const STATES: [JobState; 4] = [
+    JobState::Queued,
+    JobState::Running,
+    JobState::Succeeded,
+    JobState::Failed,
+];
+
+/// Every event, both spellings of the two events that carry a flag included.
+pub const EVENTS: [Event; 6] = [
+    Event::Claim,
+    Event::Complete,
+    Event::Fail { retryable: true },
+    Event::Fail { retryable: false },
+    Event::ClaimLapsed { retryable: true },
+    Event::ClaimLapsed { retryable: false },
+];
+
+/// Why a move was not allowed.
+///
+/// Three reasons rather than one, because a caller that treats "this job is finished" as "somebody
+/// else is running it" is a caller that retries work that is done. The class is written down so
+/// that the distinction survives in a `match` instead of living in a message somebody has to read.
+#[non_exhaustive]
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
+pub enum Reason {
+    /// The job is finished. Nothing happens after the end, whatever the event is.
+    Terminal,
+    /// The event is about a running job, and this one has not been claimed.
+    NotRunning,
+    /// The event is about a waiting job, and this one has already been claimed.
+    NotQueued,
+}
+
+impl fmt::Display for Reason {
+    fn fmt(&self, formatter: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
+        let sentence = match self {
+            Reason::Terminal => "nothing happens after the end of a job",
+            Reason::NotRunning => "the event needs the job to be running",
+            Reason::NotQueued => "the event needs the job to be waiting",
+        };
+        formatter.write_str(sentence)
+    }
+}
+
+/// A move the machine does not allow, named on both sides.
+///
+/// `from` and `event` are what the caller has in its hand, and `reason` is the class: a refusal that
+/// said only "not allowed" would leave the operator to work out which of the three things it was.
+/// The [`fmt::Display`] sentence carries all three, because it is what ends up in a log line.
+#[non_exhaustive]
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Illegal {
+    /// The state the job was in.
+    from: JobState,
+    /// The event that arrived.
+    event: Event,
+    /// Why it was refused.
+    reason: Reason,
+}
+
+impl Illegal {
+    /// A refusal, built where the machine decides one.
+    fn new(from: JobState, event: Event, reason: Reason) -> Self {
+        Self {
+            from,
+            event,
+            reason,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// The state the job was in when the event arrived.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn from(&self) -> JobState {
+        self.from
+    }
+
+    /// The event that arrived.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn event(&self) -> Event {
+        self.event
+    }
+
+    /// Why the move was refused, as a class a caller can match on.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn reason(&self) -> Reason {
+        self.reason
+    }
+}
+
+impl fmt::Display for Illegal {
+    fn fmt(&self, formatter: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
+        // Both sides are named, and the reason is a sentence rather than a code: this is the line an
+        // operator reads in a log, next to the job id the caller already has.
+        write!(
+            formatter,
+            "a job that is `{}` cannot take the event `{}`: {}",
+            self.from, self.event, self.reason
+        )
+    }
+}
+
+impl std::error::Error for Illegal {
+    // A refusal is the whole failure: there is no chain behind it, and `source()` answering `None`
+    // is the honest answer. The `Error` impl is what lets a caller that collects failures of its
+    // own (`Box<dyn Error>`, an `anyhow::Error`) carry this one without a wrapper type.
+    fn source(&self) -> Option<&(dyn std::error::Error + 'static)> {
+        None
+    }
+}
+
+/// A `state` column that holds a name this build does not know.
+///
+/// The text is kept exactly as the row held it, because that string is what an operator has to
+/// grep for: a refusal that said only "unknown state" would have thrown away the one fact that
+/// says which version wrote the row.
+#[non_exhaustive]
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct UnknownState {
+    /// Exactly what the column held.
+    text: String,
+}
+
+impl UnknownState {
+    /// A refusal for the text that was stored.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn new(text: impl Into<String>) -> Self {
+        Self { text: text.into() }
+    }
+
+    /// Exactly what the column held.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn text(&self) -> &str {
+        &self.text
+    }
+}
+
+impl fmt::Display for UnknownState {
+    fn fmt(&self, formatter: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
+        write!(
+            formatter,
+            "`{}` is not a state this engine knows",
+            self.text
+        )
+    }
+}
+
+impl std::error::Error for UnknownState {
+    // The same argument as `Illegal`: the value is the failure.
+    fn source(&self) -> Option<&(dyn std::error::Error + 'static)> {
+        None
+    }
+}
+
+impl JobState {
+    /// Read a state out of the column that holds it.
+    ///
+    /// The inverse of [`JobState::as_str`], and the reason the column is called an *encoding*: every
+    /// state the model has, the column can hold, and anything else is refused by the text that was
+    /// stored rather than rounded down. Refused rather than defaulted is the whole point — a value
+    /// the model does not know is a bug or a version skew, and turning it into `queued` is how a job
+    /// that finished is run a second time.
+    ///
+    /// # Errors
+    ///
+    /// [`UnknownState`] for anything but the four names, case included: `Queued` and `queued` are
+    /// two different strings, and the column, the CLI and a dashboard group by the second.
+    pub fn parse(text: &str) -> Result<Self, UnknownState> {
+        todo!(
+            "jobq/3.14-job-state-machine: the way back out of the column — every state `as_str` \
+             can write, and nothing else"
+        )
+    }
+}
+
+/// What happens to a job when `event` occurs while it is in `state`.
+///
+/// Total on purpose: every state and every event has an answer, and the answer for a move the
+/// machine does not allow is an [`Illegal`] naming the reason. A caller that has to handle three
+/// distinct refusals cannot accidentally treat "already finished" as "somebody else has it".
+///
+/// # Errors
+///
+/// [`Illegal`], as the table in this module's documentation says: the job is finished, the event
+/// needed it to be running, or the event needed it to be waiting.
+pub fn transition(state: JobState, event: Event) -> Result<JobState, Illegal> {
+    todo!("jobq/3.14-job-state-machine: the table in the step's body, as one total function")
+}
